I'll implement a `chess_board` class following the standard Exercism C++ track interface: constructed with white and black positions (pairs of row, column), with `white()`, `black()`, `can_attack()` methods, and a stream output operator for the board. Invalid construction (same position or out of range) throws `std::domain_error`.

queen_attack.h
```cpp
#if !defined(QUEEN_ATTACK_H)
#define QUEEN_ATTACK_H

#include <ostream>
#include <string>
#include <utility>

namespace queen_attack {

using position = std::pair<int, int>;

class chess_board {
   public:
    chess_board(const position& white, const position& black);
    chess_board(const position& white = position{0, 3},
                const position& black = position{7, 3});

    position white() const;
    position black() const;
    bool can_attack() const;

    explicit operator std::string() const;

   private:
    position white_;
    position black_;
};

std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const chess_board& board);

}  // namespace queen_attack

#endif // QUEEN_ATTACK_H
```

queen_attack.cpp
```cpp
#include "queen_attack.h"

#include <cstdlib>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace queen_attack {

namespace {
bool valid(const position& p) {
    return p.first >= 0 && p.first < 8 && p.second >= 0 && p.second < 8;
}
}  // namespace

chess_board::chess_board(const position& white, const position& black)
    : white_(white), black_(black) {
    if (!valid(white) || !valid(black)) {
        throw std::domain_error("Queen position off the board");
    }
    if (white == black) {
        throw std::domain_error("Queens cannot share the same position");
    }
}

position chess_board::white() const { return white_; }

position chess_board::black() const { return black_; }

bool chess_board::can_attack() const {
    int dr = std::abs(white_.first - black_.first);
    int dc = std::abs(white_.second - black_.second);
    return dr == 0 || dc == 0 || dr == dc;
}

chess_board::operator std::string() const {
    std::string result;
    for (int r = 0; r < 8; ++r) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 8; ++c) {
            position p{r, c};
            char ch = '_';
            if (p == white_) {
                ch = 'W';
            } else if (p == black_) {
                ch = 'B';
            }
            result += ch;
            result += (c == 7) ? '\n' : ' ';
        }
    }
    return result;
}

std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const chess_board& board) {
    return os << static_cast<std::string>(board);
}

}  // namespace queen_attack
```

I implemented a `chess_board` class in the `queen_attack` namespace. The tests weren't visible, so its interface follows what I remember of the Exercism C++ queen-attack tests. Please run them to confirm.

- **Positions:** a position is a `std::pair<int, int>`, read as (row, column).
- **Validation:** the constructor throws `std::domain_error` if a position is off the 8x8 board or both queens share a square.
- **Defaults:** the default constructor puts white at (0, 3) and black at (7, 3).
- **Accessors:** `white()` and `black()` return the two positions.
- **Attack check:** `can_attack()` is true when the queens share a row, a column, or a diagonal.
- **Board output:** `operator std::string()` draws the board, with `W` and `B` for the queens and `_` for empty squares. Squares are separated by spaces and each row ends with a newline.

