- **`queen_attack.h`**: declares `position` (a `std::pair<int, int>` of zero-indexed `{row, column}`) and a `chess_board` class. The class has:
  - a constructor taking the white and black queen positions
  - a default constructor placing white at `(0, 3)` and black at `(7, 3)`
  - `white()` and `black()` accessors
  - `can_attack()`
  - a conversion to `std::string` that renders the board
- **`queen_attack.cpp`**:
  - The constructor throws `std::domain_error` if either queen is off the 8×8 board (negative or ≥ 8) or if both queens are on the same square.
  - `can_attack()` returns true when the queens share a row, share a column, or have equal row and column distances (a diagonal).
  - The string form has one line per row, with `W`, `B` or `_` separated by spaces. Each line, including the last, ends with a newline.

queen_attack.h
```
#if !defined(QUEEN_ATTACK_H)
#define QUEEN_ATTACK_H

#include <string>
#include <utility>

namespace queen_attack {

// A square on the board as {row, column}, both zero-indexed.
using position = std::pair<int, int>;

class chess_board {
   public:
    // White queen at {0, 3}, black queen at {7, 3}.
    chess_board();

    // Throws std::domain_error if either queen is not on the board or if
    // both queens occupy the same square.
    chess_board(const position& white_queen, const position& black_queen);

    position white() const;
    position black() const;

    // True if the queens share a row, a column or a diagonal.
    bool can_attack() const;

    // Renders the board, one line per row, e.g. "_ _ _ _ W _ _ _\n".
    operator std::string() const;

   private:
    position white_;
    position black_;
};

}  // namespace queen_attack

#endif  // QUEEN_ATTACK_H
```

queen_attack.cpp
```
#include "queen_attack.h"

#include <cstdlib>
#include <stdexcept>

namespace queen_attack {

namespace {

constexpr int board_size = 8;

bool is_on_board(const position& pos) {
    return pos.first >= 0 && pos.first < board_size && pos.second >= 0 &&
           pos.second < board_size;
}

}  // namespace

chess_board::chess_board() : chess_board(position{0, 3}, position{7, 3}) {}

chess_board::chess_board(const position& white_queen,
                         const position& black_queen)
    : white_(white_queen), black_(black_queen) {
    if (!is_on_board(white_) || !is_on_board(black_)) {
        throw std::domain_error("Queens must be placed on the board");
    }
    if (white_ == black_) {
        throw std::domain_error("Queens cannot occupy the same square");
    }
}

position chess_board::white() const { return white_; }

position chess_board::black() const { return black_; }

bool chess_board::can_attack() const {
    const int row_distance = std::abs(white_.first - black_.first);
    const int column_distance = std::abs(white_.second - black_.second);

    return row_distance == 0 || column_distance == 0 ||
           row_distance == column_distance;
}

chess_board::operator std::string() const {
    std::string board;

    for (int row = 0; row < board_size; ++row) {
        for (int column = 0; column < board_size; ++column) {
            const position square{row, column};

            if (square == white_) {
                board += 'W';
            } else if (square == black_) {
                board += 'B';
            } else {
                board += '_';
            }

            board += (column + 1 < board_size) ? ' ' : '\n';
        }
    }

    return board;
}

}  // namespace queen_attack
```

I'm assuming the standard Exercism C++ interface for this exercise: a `chess_board` class constructed from two `std::pair<int,int>` positions, with `white()`, `black()` and `can_attack()` methods and an `operator std::string()` that renders the board. The constructor throws `std::domain_error` if both queens share a position.

I also made the two helpers `const` and added a conversion to string for the board display. The conversion is rarely needed, but the Exercism tests sometimes use it.

